{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the given system of equations involving complex numbers, we start with the first equation:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 4}{z - 8} \\right| = 1.\n\\]\n\nThis implies that the magnitudes of the numerator and the denominator are equal, so we can write:\n\n\\[\n|z - 4| = |z - 8|.\n\\]\n\nThis means that the point \\(z\\) is equidistant from the points 4 and 8 on the complex plane. The locus of points equidistant from two fixed points is the perpendicular bisector of the line segment joining these points. The midpoint of 4 and 8 is 6, and the perpendicular bisector is the vertical line \\( \\text{Re}(z) = 6 \\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\nz = 6 + bi,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(b\\) is a real number. Now, we substitute \\(z = 6 + bi\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 12}{z - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(z = 6 + bi\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{6 + bi - 12}{6 + bi - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3} \\implies \\left| \\frac{-6 + bi}{6 + (b-8)i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of a complex number \\(\\frac{a+ci}{d+ei}\\) is \\(\\frac{|a+ci|}{|d+ei|}\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|-6 + bi|}{|6 + (b-8)i|} = \\frac{5}{3} \\implies \\frac{\\sqrt{36 + b^2}}{\\sqrt{36 + (b-8)^2}} = \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1184,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,2661,1849,315,37906,15860,6351,5109,11,582,1191,448,279,1156,23606,1447,59,9640,59,2359,91,1124,37018,90,89,481,220,19,15170,89,481,220,23,92,1124,1291,91,284,220,16,624,59,2533,1986,23945,429,279,8455,20590,315,279,63533,323,279,46912,525,6144,11,773,582,646,3270,1447,59,9640,91,89,481,220,19,91,284,760,89,481,220,23,26280,59,2533,1986,3363,429,279,1459,17767,89,57758,374,3232,307,11202,504,279,3501,220,19,323,220,23,389,279,6351,11031,13,576,78157,315,3501,3232,307,11202,504,1378,8356,3501,374,2