ht| &= 1, \\\\\n\\left| \\frac{z - 12}{z - 8i} \\right| &= \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\end{align*}Enter all the solutions, separated by commas.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5618e45fbb00d0bfe32a1ab22abc3548de9cb2e55a52ab701bfcaafcf1d1ea4c"},"seconds":26.510998964309692,"seed":495158880372249733,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given system of equations involving complex numbers, we start with the first equation:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 4}{z - 8} \\right| = 1.\n\\]\n\nThis implies that the magnitudes of the numerator and the denominator are equal, so we can write:\n\n\\[\n|z - 4| = |z - 8|.\n\\]\n\nThis means that the point \\(z\\) is equidistant from the points 4 and 8 on the complex plane. The locus of points equidistant from two fixed points is the perpendicular bisector of the line segment joining these points. The midpoint of 4 and 8 is 6, and the perpendicular bisector is the vertical line \\( \\text{Re}(z) = 6 \\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\nz = 6 + bi,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(b\\) is a real number. Now, we substitute \\(z = 6 + bi\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{z - 12}{z - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(z = 6 + bi\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{6 + bi - 12}{6 + bi - 8i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3} \\implies \\left| \\frac{-6 + bi}{6 + (b-8)i} \\right| = \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of a complex number \\(\\frac{a+ci}{d+ei}\\) is \\(\\frac{|a+ci|}{|d+ei|}\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|-6 + bi|}{|6 + (b-8)i|} = \\frac{5}{3} \\implies \\frac{\\sqrt{36 + b^2}}{\\sqrt{36 + (b-8)^2}} = \\frac{5}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36 + b^2}{36 + (b-8)^2} = \\frac{25}{9}.\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\n\\[\n9(36 + b^2) = 25(36 + (b-8)^2) \\implies 324 + 9b^2 = 900 + 25(b^2 - 16b + 64).\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\n\\[\n324 + 9b^2 = 900 + 25b^2 - 400b + 1600 \\implies 324 + 9b^2 = 2500 + 25b^2 - 400b.\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n0 = 16b^2 - 400b + 1876 \\implies 4b^2 - 100b + 469 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 4\\), \\(B = -100\\), and \\(C = 469\\):\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{100 \\pm \\sqrt{10000 - 7504}}{8} = \\frac{100 \\pm \\sqrt{2496}}{8} = \\frac{100 \\pm 4\\sqrt{156}}{8} = \\frac{100 \\pm 4 \\cdot 2\\sqrt{39}}{8} = \\frac{100 \\pm 8\\sqrt{39}}{8} = \\frac{25 \\pm 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\(b\\) are \\(b = \\frac{25 + 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}\\) and \\(b = \\frac{25 - 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}\\). Therefore, the solutions for \\(z\\) are:\n\n\\[\nz = 6 + \\frac{25 + 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}i \\quad \\text{and} \\quad z = 6 + \\frac{25 - 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}i.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6 + \\frac{25 + 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}i, 6 + \\frac{25 - 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}i}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6 + \\frac{25 + 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}i, 6 + \\frac{25 - 2\\sqrt{39}}{2}i}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:888"}
